    not_duplicate_of: []  # 097c
    pr_ref:  # 097c
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-11 during the WI-bahid investigation. WI-bahid asks about Go's 33\nthird-party rows. That framing is too small: Go is the FOURTH-largest violator of\nfive, and fixing Go alone would leave the shipped claim false while making it\nfalse in a more specific and therefore more misleading way.\n\nTHE CENSUS (via load_catalog over all 14 shipped catalogues):\n\n    elixir   155 / 528  (29.4%)  Ecto 40, Phoenix 28, HTTPoison 16, Req 15,\n                                 Tesla 15, Plug.Conn 12, Mint 9, Postgrex 5,\n                                 Redix 4, MyXQL 4, Finch 3, Oban 3\n    haskell   68 / 216  (31.5%)  (48 on a stricter line that excludes\n                                 bytestring/text/network)\n    swift     38 / 104  (36.5%)  SwiftNIO family 21, swift-log 8,\n                                 AsyncHTTPClient, NIOSSL, WebSocket, Tracing\n    go        33 / 190  (17.4%)\n    python    33 / 222           BUT 29 are the DOCUMENTED, TEST-ENFORCED\n                                 django.db.models carve-out; the ungoverned\n                                 residue is 4 rows (aiohttp.web, flask.Flask,\n                                 ujson, uvicorn) inside a `status: complete`\n                                 catalogue whose own 300-entry stdlib_modules\n                                 list refutes them.\n\nTHREE CATALOGUE HEADERS ALREADY DECLARE THIRD-PARTY SCOPE ON PURPOSE, which means\nthis is not uniformly drift — it is an unratified policy some catalogues have\nadopted openly: swift.yaml:3-5 (\"SwiftNIO, AsyncHTTPClient, NIOSSL\"),\nhaskell.yaml:4 (\"and common libraries\"), objc.yaml:4 (\"common framework APIs\").\nelixir.yaml:11-14 goes furthest, naming \"a galaxy of HTTP clients (HTTPoison,\nTesla, Req, Finch, Mint)\" and justifying it by a UAT bug report (BUG-09b) — an\nunbounded, unwritten, ungated carve-out at 4.7x Go's row count, granted on the\nidentical recall argument that WI-bahid is weighing for Go.\n\nSCOPE GATES EXIST FOR 6 OF 14 LANGUAGES, AND THAT ASYMMETRY IS THE MECHANICAL\nREASON THE OTHERS DRIFTED:\n    universal loops   java (test_io_boundary.py:253-262), kotlin (:319-329),\n                      scala (:337-347)\n    targeted denylist python (:143-168), javascript (:277-296), rust (:302-310)\n    NONE              go, elixir, erlang, haskell, swift, objc, c, cpp\n(test_go_web_framework_overlay.py:83-90, which I added under INV-safig, is a\n6-module parametrized denylist, not a scope loop — it names the rows it knows\nabout and cannot catch the next one.)\n\nTHERE ARE ALREADY TWO CARVE-OUT SHAPES IN THE TREE, both legitimate and both\nprecedent: a NAMESPACE-PREFIX whitelist (java's javax.*/jakarta.*, 26 rows,\ntest-enforced, inherited by kotlin and scala) and a PER-ROW admission criterion\nwith written conditions (python.yaml:182-200, django.db.models, which states\noutright that the rule is \"not a purity ban\"). A third shape invented for one\nlanguage would formalise per-language divergence rather than remove it.\n\nSO THE HONEST QUESTION IS PROJECT-WIDE: is the real policy \"stdlib only\" or\n\"stdlib plus documented, gated carve-outs\"? The tree currently practises the\nsecond while every user-facing string asserts the first. Whichever is ratified,\nthe deliverable is the same shape: one written rule, one gate shape applied to\nALL 14 catalogues, and every asserting string updated together.\n\n~15 SITES ASSERT THE STDLIB-ONLY CLAIM and would all have to move together — a\nGo-only edit turns most of them into false quotations of an ADR that no longer\nsays what they quote: cli.py:8845 and :8944 (the language-generic --io-primitives\nhelp text), scripts/generate-security-md:163-168 AND the committed SECURITY.md:155,\ngo.yaml:4, io_boundary.py:897 and :951-952, python.yaml:17-18,\ndocs/io-primitives-overlays/go-web-frameworks.yaml:8-10 and\npython-http-clients.yaml:9, test_go_web_framework_overlay.py:9-10 and :89,\ntest_io_primitives_overlay.py:5-6 and :131, CHANGELOG.md:17 and :123,\ndocs/hypergumbo-self-catalog/README.md:25.\n\nUNPRICED, AND IT IS WHAT DECIDES AFFORDABILITY: no chain-cost measurement exists\nfor elixir, haskell, swift or python's residue, in the tracker or the bakeoff\nartifacts. Elixir is the one that matters — its own header says it exists because\na UAT reported ZERO boundaries on a Phoenix/Ecto repo, so its cull cost is\nplausibly an order of magnitude above Go's 111. Any decision to generalise\nWI-bahid's ruling is uninformed until that is measured.\n\nBLOCKED ON WI-bahid only in ordering, not in substance: Go's answer should not be\nallowed to stand as the project's answer by default."  # 097c
    fields:  # 097c
      statement: "One catalogue-scope rule, one gate shape applied to every language, and every asserting string agreeing with it."  # 097c
