- op: discuss  # 061a
  at: '2026-08-28T16:10:49Z'  # 061a
  by: agent  # 061a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 061a
  clock: 4  # 061a
  nonce: 061a  # 061a
  message: "NOT UP TO DATE. Re-checked 2026-08-28 against dev e60cc88f5d; every denominator\nin the census has moved, and one of the two causes is my own work today.\n\nMEASURED NOW vs THE 2026-08-11 CENSUS (total rows per catalogue, via\nload_catalog, include_defaults=False):\n\n    elixir    528 -> 529   (+1)\n    haskell   216 -> 179   (-37)\n    swift     104 -> 107   (+3)\n    go        190 -> 193   (+3)\n    python    222 -> 464   (+242)\n\nTWO CAUSES, and they want different treatment.\n\n(1) PYTHON'S +242 PREDATES ME AND IS THE BIG ONE. The 2026-08-15 stdlib climb\n    landed four days after this item was filed and more than doubled the\n    catalogue. Any conclusion drawn from \"python 33/222\" is about a catalogue\n    that no longer exists, and the 4-row ungoverned residue (aiohttp.web,\n    flask.Flask, ujson, uvicorn) needs re-confirming against the current file\n    rather than being carried forward.\n\n(2) HASKELL'S -37 IS MINE, TODAY (INV-nular, PRs #602/#606). Removed: the 31\n    in-process db_* rows (Data.IORef / Data.STRef / Control.Concurrent.MVar /\n    Control.Concurrent.STM), plus Network.Socket socket/bind/listen/close/\n    gracefulClose. FIVE OF THOSE ARE THIRD-PARTY BY THIS ITEM'S OWN LINE --\n    Network.Socket is the `network` package, which the item's stricter haskell\n    count explicitly excludes. So the third-party numerator moved too, not just\n    the denominator.\n\nALSO IN SCOPE AND NOT A REMOVAL: PR #609 RE-DECLARED 26 THIRD-PARTY elixir ROWS\nwithout changing their count. Phoenix.Controller / Plug.Conn / Phoenix.Channel /\nPhoenix.Endpoint response helpers moved net_recv -> net_send (they SEND; every\none was declared a receive, so a Phoenix app had no egress surface at all).\nAnyone auditing \"which third-party rows ship, under which boundary\" must know\nthose 26 changed boundary on 08-28 even though the row count did not.\n\nTHE CENSUS IS NOT MECHANICALLY REPRODUCIBLE, WHICH IS THE REAL OBSTACLE TO\nCLOSING THIS. I tried to re-derive the numerators with\n`IoBoundaryCatalog.is_stdlib_module` and got 100.0% third-party for elixir,\nhaskell, swift AND go -- because only python declares a `stdlib_modules` list,\nso the predicate returns False for every module in the other four. That is\nRECOGNITION, not provenance (the INV-buzab distinction), and here it produces a\nnumber that is not merely imprecise but meaningless.\n\nSo the 155 / 68 / 38 / 33 figures rest on a HAND-CURATED module classification\nthat exists nowhere in the repo -- only as the family lists in this description\n(Ecto, Phoenix, HTTPoison, Req, Tesla, Mint, Postgrex, Redix, MyXQL, Finch,\nOban; SwiftNIO, swift-log, AsyncHTTPClient, NIOSSL; bytestring/text/network).\nWhoever takes this item cannot re-measure drift without reconstructing that\nlist, and it will go stale again the same way.\n\nRECOMMENDED FIRST STEP, ahead of any cull: make the census reproducible. Either\ngive each catalogue a `stdlib_modules` enumeration (python already has one, and\nthat is exactly why python is the only language whose numerator survived this\ncheck), or commit the curated third-party module list beside the catalogues so\n`is_stdlib_module`'s failure mode stops being invisible. A cull decided against\nnumbers nobody can recompute will be undone by the next stdlib climb.\n\nNOT CHANGING STATUS: still todo_hard, still P1, and the item's THESIS is\nuntouched -- five catalogues ship third-party rows while user-facing strings\nassert stdlib-only, and scope gates still exist for only some languages. Only\nthe arithmetic is stale."  # 061a
- op: discuss  # f5ff
  at: '2026-08-28T16:14:47Z'  # f5ff
  by: agent  # f5ff
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f5ff
  clock: 5  # f5ff
  nonce: f5ff  # f5ff
  message: "CORRECTION TO MY PREVIOUS NOTE. I recommended \"make the census reproducible\nahead of any cull\". That re-opened a question that is already decided, and I am\nwithdrawing it.\n\nADR-0047 RULING 1 (accepted 08-27) DISPOSES OF THIS ITEM'S ROWS: \"The shipped\ncatalogues stay stdlib-only ... hypergumbo may additionally ship community\noverlays it does not vouch for, which are loaded by default and disclosed as\nunvouched. THE THIRD-PARTY ROWS NOW IN FIVE CATALOGUES MOVE INTO THOSE OVERLAYS,\ndjango.db.models included.\" So the disposition is not open, and this item is a\nFILING that the ADR answers rather than a design question awaiting one.\n\nWI-surun executes it (rulings 1/8) and names this item in its own CLOSES line.\nIts dependency -- \"the default-overlay leg landing first, or the cull strands\nthe rows\" -- was satisfied today by WI-riros (#598), so WI-surun is UNBLOCKED.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES FROM MY NOTE, and I have moved it to WI-surun where it is\nactionable: the census numbers are stale (measured deltas there), and\n`is_stdlib_module` cannot identify which rows to move -- it returns 100%\nthird-party for elixir, haskell, swift AND go because only python declares a\n`stdlib_modules` list. That is an obstacle to EXECUTING the decided migration,\nnot an argument for a new prerequisite, and framing it as the latter was my\nerror.\n\nThis is the failure my own operating notes put first -- the ruling already\nexisted and I wrote a recommendation instead of reading it. Recording it here\nrather than quietly deleting the note."  # f5ff
